 of engagement with the office and  the work that I was doing now in this  story it would be really easy to say  that the root issue here the root cause  the thing that we should be focusing on  fixing was the code review process and  that it was flawed and that may not  necessarily be wrong but I don't think  it's the root problem oftentimes when  things go wrong we want to point to  something that's really easy to fix  and so what happens is we often fix a  bunch of symptoms and we wind up with a  whole lot of processes rules and  guidelines but root frustrations for our  developers and for our team are often  still there when it comes to building a  great team we need to make sure we're  fixing those deep problems those root  frustrations right we could have fixed  the speed problem of the code reviews by  mandating that every code review be done  in a certain amount of time and we could  have written some code and some bots to  help enforce those time limits but what  would have happened  myself and the other junior developers  on the team probably would have felt  like we were just being a burden to  those senior engineers on the team right  because we're now dictating what they're  spending their time on and that's not a  healthy environment to be in  so that route frustration of being  unsure if my contribution actually  mattered would still be boiling just  under the surface so in this story the  real root problem here is that my team  wasn't good at sharing encouragement and  positive feedback now ironically to  point that out to someone you have to  give them some negative feedback but no  one was willing to actually go that far  and so what happened is I had no signal  that the work I was doing was good but I  had a pretty strong signal that my  contributions weren't that valued so the  answer here is that more process it  rarely ever is often times we can solve  technical problems through better  process or improved workflows but rarely  does that actually help us with  interpersonal problems right instead we  need to focus on fixing these sort of  issues primarily by communicating better  communicating carefully apply some  purpose some empathy to how you  communicate with your team maybe you see  your junior developer and they're  struggling with the code review process  and so be sure that had some positive  comments I let them know they're doing a  good job even if they're having lots of  issues with the code they're submitting  okay or you interact with a team lead  and you see that they're really stressed  out maybe let them know that your  request wasn't that urgent or if you can  offer to take some other workload off  the idea is that our communication both  in our team as well as the larger  software engineering community were in  should always have this constant purpose  of trying to build a better environment  one where relationships are solid and  people actually feel meaningfully  connected to the work that they're doing  imagine the impact these sorts of things  would have on the open-source community  right maybe new contributors would feel  more welcomed and actually want to get  involved more and maintainer x' might  not feel so overwhelmed and burnout is  frequently this idea of communicating  carefully is really just a subset of a  larger idea that I want to talk about  and that is mindfulness which is this  practice of paying attention in a  particular way on purpose in the present  moment and non-judgmentally purposeful  non-judgmental attention to what is  going on around you it's very similar to  what I was just talking about right but  as you might guess from the definition  that I just gave it's a relatively broad  subject but at its core mindfulness is  simply about training yourself to really  engage with what is going on around you  which is why it's often defined by  practices like yoga or meditation other  very structured very purposeful  activities right but maybe you're like  me and 