racted right  what that conveys to you is whatever  you're saying isn't that important if it  happens repeatedly you start to  internalize this those feelings no  longer is it the thing I'm talking about  isn't important it starts to become I'm  not important and that's really  dangerous if you're trying to build a  cohesive collaborative environment so  try to be mindful be purposeful practice  empathy when you communicate applying  these lessons to all spheres of software  development is only going to make it  much more inclusive and welcoming to  everyone instead of the culture that  many of us experience which is filled  with fear and insecurity fear in  particular I think is a really  compelling reason for us to strive  towards building safe teams because fear  is a huge enemy of innovation in  creativity which brings us to my next  story so on my second job I got to work  on an important piece of infrastructure  for a very large application now I was  still relatively junior at the time and  so I solicited advice from senior  engineers and even some design  committees before deciding to move  forward will we move forward I was super  proud of my work these people I  respected these senior engineers had  said this seems like a good idea  and that was wonderful  but when performance review time came  around it was a different story there  are some problems with the project and  they were ultimately used as  justification to not give me a promotion  now obviously that's a huge bummer but  the real frustrating part was that they  had left comments stating that I had  acted too independently that I should  have involved more senior engineers but  that's what I thought that I had done  and this experience led me to avoid  responsibility in the future I didn't  want to take responsibility be the point  person if all the blame for what I  thought was a team decision was just  going to come back on me eventually due  to that and some other reasons I left  the organization entirely now as before  it would be easy to find a solution to  this problem right you might say oh you  should have got that promotion but the  promotion is just a distraction the real  problem here is that the reality of the  work that I was doing wasn't getting  properly communicated to the people that  could make decisions about it and what  that told me is that this team wasn't  safe for me to take risks on I had to be  careful about the work that I chose to  pursue to make sure that perceptions  wouldn't be skewed and that I would be  reflected the way I wanted people to  know me so this problem is known as  impression management which is this idea  that you have to act a certain way to  ensure a favorable perception of  yourself for other people basically you  need to manipulate them in order to feel  safe we see this all the time in small  ways right maybe you don't ask that  question in your team meeting because  you don't want to look stupid or maybe  you don't challenge that idea that you  think is bad because that might hurt  your chances of getting a promotion  right these sorts of behaviors are  poison to building good collaborative  teams because everyone's more worried  about the impression that they're  leaving than they are about the actual  work that they're doing and that's  really at the heart of what is wrong in  the story I just shared no longer wasn't  willing to take risks to be responsible  because I was concerned about the  impression I was going to leave this  sort of fear leads teams  stagnation and to potentially ruin and  it does it in much the same way as  technical debt and your project does  when you have lots of tech debt in your  project as I'm sure many of us have  experienced especially because we work  on CSS it becomes difficult or in some  cases impossible to make any sort of  major changes and even small ones become  risky right in the very same way when  you have a culture of impression  management and fear of openly sharing  ideas with each other it becomes  difficult and in some cases truly  impossible to enact any sort of lasting  change so how do we combat this how do  we combat this type of culture well we  have to strive in the opposite direction  to build a culture where you can show up  and fully employ yourself in your work  without being worried of negative  consequences to your self-image to your  status or even to your career the  separate environment is known as being a  psychologically safe one and it stands  in direct contrast to that team where  you have to be scared of the impression  you're leaving psychologically safe  teams they feel comfortable taking  interpersonal risks which are things  like suggesting new potentially  controversial ideas to solving problems  or actually acknowledging and owning up  to your failures but then actually  learning from them their risks because  you could find yourself in a position  where you're embarrassed or maybe even  actually punished for your actions by  your team the sort of team though where  you can take these risks give everyone a  chance to contribute their voice to  discussion right they're cognizant of  how they're interacting with one another  and how it affects each other  and research from major players like  Google and even Harvard has shown that  these teams they learn more quickly  they're more innovative and they perform  better overall but how do we develop  this right how do we get to a place  where we're comfortable taking  interpersonal risks with each other the  short answer is you have to start by  experimenting and learning rapidly and I  like to think about interpersonal risks  like software deployments  if you have an effective continuous  deployment setup then the impact of any  one bad commit that goes out is usually  small right if an issue occurs you get  feedback about it real quick you make  corrections you deploy again in a  similar fashion your team should have a  methodology of continuously learning  right if someone makes a mistake  give them quick and timely feedback you  make corrections as needed and you move  forward you don't dwell on issues  unnecessarily and you don't save  feedback for a later date otherwise that  just forgets a culture of scorekeeping  where everyone is unsure of who's  tracking their every move in my story if  I had received feedback as soon as  someone noticed there was a problem we  could have dealt with it a lot more  effectively right we could have learned  from it instead I only received the  feedback as I was being denied a  promotion that doesn't communicate that  there's an opportunity to learn and it  also doesn't communicate that their  safety and taking risks on your projects  all that it really communicates is that  there are penalties for the actions that  you have so the critical point here is  you have to give feedback feedback helps  us build trust and lets us avoid  additional process and it develops  better working relationships between the  people that are involved in it and we  can use those pieces to help us inform  our future decisions to learn faster and  be better as individuals as a team and  as a whole community so give feedback  give it early give it transparently and  give it often to each other learn  rapidly from your failures don't be  afraid of that and be courageous you  have to be courageous to step out to try  to build this culture in your workplace  now there's one last point to hit on and  this realm of taking risks how do you  generate effective feedback because as  we've seen it's really easy for us to  oversimplify solutions to problems or  identify the wrong problem so I'm a big  fan of this model called the five lies  the idea is simple when you're presented  with a problem you repeatedly ask the  question why  why is this happening why did that  happen until you reach an actual root  cause that you should fix  so as a brief example let's consider the  scenario where I push some broken CSS to  production  maybe our site looks like this my  product manager comes over and says why  does the site look weird well I put some  bad CSS why did you push bad CSS well we  didn't test it thoroughly  why didn't we test it thoroughly well we  needed the ship to hit the deadline why  didn't the deadline include time to  properly QA or to test it we did  included in the estimate why didn't we  include it in the estimate  well we didn't consider it to be part of  delivering a future maybe we should  change that right if we have stopped  after the first question here all the  blame would have just been placed on my  shoulders and we would have been done  with it and that leaves developers  feeling unsafe in their position and  that's often what we do unfortunately  because we want the easy fix the quick  fix so the five why's approach here has  this direct benefit of letting you  identify deep issues in your culture the  way you approach working but it also  lets you build a safe team where people  can take smart risks because mistakes  get treated as gaps in your tooling or  your training or your technology not  necessarily as personal failures which  makes it safe for you to take those  smart risks there are two quotes I think  really hammer home this point the first  is that you must never place a worker  only one defense away from failure and  the reason for this is because people  aren't really afraid of failure they're  afraid of being blamed we're all going  to fail at some point it's human nature  some of us like myself might even fail  multiple times in a single day on  occasion accidents and mistakes they  happen but if you spend every moment of  your job in your life worrying about  that you're just never going to do  anything super noteworthy and that is  one unique reason why psychological  safety is so important for those of us  involved in a creative technological  industry it's because it lets us take  risks with the technology with the tools  